+**Standards and mature engineering foundations** provide the strongest stable base. ISO/IEC/IEEE 29148:2018 covers requirements engineering; ISO/IEC 25010:2023 provides a contemporary software-product quality model; EARS provides a lightweight structured requirements notation; Cucumber documents executable BDD specifications; Eiffel documents Design by Contract; and OpenAPI provides a standardized, language-agnostic machine-readable API description. [ISO29148], [ISO25010], [EARS], [GHERKIN], [EIFFEL], [OPENAPI]
+
+**Primary tool documentation** is the best source for what current SDD products actually do, but not for proving their claimed benefits. The most relevant current sources are GitHub Spec Kit’s methodology and agentic-SDD documentation, Kiro’s specifications/steering/correctness documentation, and OpenSpec’s default schema and configuration documentation. [SK-FLOW], [SK-PERSIST], [KI-SPECS], [KI-STEER], [KI-TEST], [OS-SCHEMA], [OS-CONFIG]
+
+**Peer-reviewed evidence directly about complete SDD workflows is limited in the sources reviewed.** Hill’s ESEM 2026 paper challenges simple causal claims about specification artifacts and explicitly records that modern SDD-tool outputs were absent from its sample. [HILL]
+
+**Preprints and scoped benchmarks provide provisional evidence.** EPIC proposes a quality framework, SpecMine documents a large corpus, and the teamwork paper proposes socio-technical patterns. SWE-CC examines policy conformance in its evaluated configurations. The Orchid conference paper measures effects of ambiguity in function-level code generation. Each finding has the scope of its own study or benchmark. [EPIC], [SPECMINE], [ORCHID], [TEAMWORK], [SWE-CC]
+
+**Industry and project material documents emerging patterns.** Augment describes coordinated parallel work, Tessl documents skills and reusable context, and Spec Kit describes customizable workflows. These sources help explain product designs and vendor claims. [AUGMENT], [TESSL-CORE], [SK-INDEX]
+
+As of **4 November 2026**, the reviewed evidence supports treating SDD as a varied family of agentic engineering practices. SpecMine documents substantial public artifact coverage. EPIC and Orchid address measurable aspects of specification quality and ambiguity. The established disciplines above explain the underlying engineering techniques. General claims that current SDD workflows improve overall quality, productivity or maintainability require stronger comparative evidence. [SPECMINE], [EPIC], [ORCHID], [HILL], [TEAMWORK]
+
+The most promising interpretation of SDD is therefore not **“write more documentation before coding.”** It is **“make intent, uncertainty, constraints, decisions and evidence durable enough that humans and increasingly autonomous software agents can reason about the same system without depending on invisible context.”** That is the part of the paradigm most strongly supported by both its historical foundations and the architecture of the current ecosystem. [ISO29148], [KI-STEER], [SK-PERSIST], [TEAMWORK]
